   "heading": "The **here** package",
   "segment_index": "S5.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:81-81",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.jp.qmd:81-81",
   "proposition": "The English says a real copy of the raw linelist is first materialised into the 'data/linelists' sub-folder with appliedepidata::save_data(), so the code below runs as written. The Japanese omits this sentence.",
   "reader_consequence": "The Japanese reader sees appliedepidata::save_data() as the first line of the code chunk with no explanation, and cannot tell that this line is what creates the file that import() then reads.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "The **here** package",
   "segment_index": "S5.P2",
   "kind": "code_mismatch",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:89-89",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.jp.qmd:89-89",
   "proposition": "The Japanese prose names the command here(\"data\", \"linelists\", \"ebola_linelist.xlsx\"), but the code chunk above it (jp:86) and the expanded absolute path below it (jp:92) both use linelist_raw.xlsx.",
   "reader_consequence": "The reader is shown a file name that appears in neither the code nor the resulting path, so the worked example does not join up and the reader may think here() resolves ebola_linelist.xlsx to a linelist_raw.xlsx path.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "\"Relative\" file paths",
   "segment_index": "S7.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:119-119",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.jp.qmd:119-119",
   "proposition": "The English instructs the reader to materialise the .xlsx file with appliedepidata::save_data() as shown above. The Japanese drops that instruction.",
   "reader_consequence": "The Japanese reader cannot reproduce the example when the linelists folder is empty, because nothing explains that the save_data() call in the chunk creates the file.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Specific Excel sheets",
   "segment_index": "S11.P1",
   "kind": "missing",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:203-203",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.jp.qmd:204-204",
   "proposition": "The English states that by default import() reads the FIRST sheet of an .xlsx workbook. The Japanese omits this default and starts directly with the which = argument.",
   "reader_consequence": "The Japanese reader does not learn what import() does when which = is absent, so they may assume every sheet is read, or that which = must always be supplied for Excel files.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Skip rows",
   "segment_index": "S13.P2",
   "kind": "untrue",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:256-256",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.jp.qmd:257-257",
   "proposition": "The English says skip = accepts one integer VALUE and not a range. The Japanese says only 1 row (1 行のみ) can be specified for skip =.",
   "reader_consequence": "A reader can take this to mean skip = may never exceed 1, and avoid skip = 2, although the Excel and CSV examples later on the same page both use skip = 2.",
   "confidence": "low"
  },
  {
   "heading": "Use the file info",
   "segment_index": "S30.P1",
   "kind": "untrue",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:646-646",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.jp.qmd:647-647",
   "proposition": "The Japanese adds the parenthetical claim that only the dplyr function pull() has this functionality. The English makes no exclusivity claim about pull().",
   "reader_consequence": "The reader believes pull() is the only way to extract a column as a vector, does not consider base R $ or [[, and may add a dplyr dependency for that reason alone.",
   "confidence": "high"
  },
  {
   "heading": "APIs",
   "segment_index": "S31.P1",
   "kind": "untrue",
   "en_span": "chapters/importing.qmd:664-664",
   "tr_span": "chapters/importing.jp.qmd:665-665",
   "proposition": "The English describes platforms with user IDs and CREDENTIALS. The Japanese renders credentials as 経歴 (career or personal history), so it describes platforms that hold user IDs and career histories.",
   "reader_consequence": "The reader loses the stated reason authentication is needed, which is that access is controlled by credentials, and reads it instead as a property of the data the platform stores.",
